ng  to vendors  and say you know i can run my sql over  here or i can run it here you both talk  to the same data set  right so thinking about things like data  gravity and a central table store you  know where do you want the gravity of  the data that you're managing  do you want that in an engine that ties  you to expensive compute or do you want  that in a place where you control it and  everything talks to that you know i  think that's a conversation we really  need to have over the next uh five years  probably  um  access control is another big one  hadoop space is  you know like you can get it if you uh  if you have a trino plug-in that  implements it but oh yeah if you're  using spark as well  you know we're just gonna  uh give total access to the warehouse um  you know you need something that  actually does protect data no matter  what is accessing it  and also the one that i tend to harp on  the most these days is like we need to  stop losing table structure how much of  a day is spent um  taking csv files from a vendor and  reconstituting table structure around it  rather than just sharing tables natively  um like snowflake can do so those are  the capabilities i think that we need to  to pay attention to and really that is i  i think of this as the multi-platform  engine is like  sorry multi-engine platform is the  challenge and thing that we need to  focus on next  um you know what goes in that space i  think clearly iceberg is in this  um this space of where you want the to  concentrate the gravity of your data  everything talks to um that format but  iceberg doesn't cover access control and  i don't really think that it should  there are a lot of things that iceberg  itself doesn't do it's not a metastore  for example you bring your own metastore  so we need to define what goes in this  box right the multi-engine platform is  the the next big challenge and i want  people hopefully to think more about  that and less about iceberg although i  would say t